mb, increasing the strain on the biological interface points—the socket, the skin, the underlying muscles. That can lead to faster physical exhaustion of the user because they don't get the "my calf is burning" warning. **So, you're entirely correct in your literal observation:** the bionic limb itself cannot suffer from muscle fatigue. But the original question about "amputees becoming less fatigued" is a question about the entire human-machine system. And in that system, the biological parts (residual musculature, brain, sound limbs) still tire, often more than they would in an able-bodied person performing the same task, because they are now powering and coordinating a device that, while tireless, is not a seamless extension of the body.
